IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `oas-core`

-- Old Age Security Act and Regulations. SHARED HELPERS: month, age, time-limit and rounding arithmetic.
--
-- Nothing in this module is a provision of the Act or the Regulations; it is the one place where the
-- arithmetic the whole text leans on is written, so that no piece writes its own and two pieces never
-- define the same name. A piece calls these rules and never redefines them.
--
-- A MONTH is the DATE of its first day. The Act pays by the month ("for any month in a payment period",
-- "the month in which the person attains 75 years of age"), so every rule that is given a month
-- normalises it first with `the first day of the month containing`.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Months

GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the first day of the month containing` d MEANS
    Date 1 (DATE_MONTH d) (DATE_YEAR d)

GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day of the month containing` d MEANS
    Date (`Days in month` (DATE_MONTH d) (DATE_YEAR d)) (DATE_MONTH d) (DATE_YEAR d)

-- A number that increases by one with each calendar month, so that two months can be compared and
-- subtracted. It has no meaning of its own: use it only through the rules below.
GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the number of the month containing` d MEANS
    (DATE_YEAR d TIMES 12) PLUS (DATE_MONTH d MINUS 1)

-- `add months` clamps the day of the month, and the first of a month always exists, so the answer is
-- the first day of the month n months on (n negative: earlier).
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the first day of the month that is` n `months after the month containing` d MEANS
    `add months` (`the first day of the month containing` d) n

-- How many calendar months b is after a (negative if before).
GIVEN a IS A DATE
      b IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the number of months from the month containing` a `to the month containing` b MEANS
    (`the number of the month containing` b) MINUS (`the number of the month containing` a)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Ages. A person attains an age on the anniversary of the birth. For a person born on 29 February the
-- anniversary in a year that is not a leap year is taken to be 28 February, because `add years`
-- clamps; the text does not say (NOTES.md, fork register).

GIVEN birth IS A DATE
      n     IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the day on which a person born on` birth `attains the age of` n MEANS
    `add years` birth n

GIVEN birth IS A DATE
      n     IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the month in which a person born on` birth `attains the age of` n MEANS
    `the first day of the month containing` (`add years` birth n)

-- The age in complete years on a day; negative if the day is before the birth.
GIVEN day   IS A DATE
      birth IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the age in complete years on` day `of a person born on` birth MEANS
    IF `add years` birth (DATE_YEAR day MINUS DATE_YEAR birth) AT MOST day
    THEN DATE_YEAR day MINUS DATE_YEAR birth
    ELSE (DATE_YEAR day MINUS DATE_YEAR birth) MINUS 1

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Time limits. See `Time limit` in oas-core. Days are counted on the calendar day by day, months and
-- years on the calendar (clamping to the last day of a shorter month), exactly as `add months` and
-- `add years` do.

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `Time limit`
`a limit in days` n MEANS
    `Time limit` WITH `number of units` IS n, unit IS Days

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `Time limit`
`a limit in months` n MEANS
    `Time limit` WITH `number of units` IS n, unit IS Months

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `Time limit`
`a limit in years` n MEANS
    `Time limit` WITH `number of units` IS n, unit IS Years

-- "within ninety days after the day on which ...": the last day on which the thing may be done.
GIVEN limit IS A `Time limit`
      start IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day that is` limit `after` start MEANS
    CONSIDER limit's unit
    WHEN Days   THEN start PLUS limit's `number of units`
    WHEN Months THEN `add months` start (limit's `number of units`)
    WHEN Years  THEN `add years` start (limit's `number of units`)

-- "not later than fifteen days before that month": the last day on which the thing may be done.
GIVEN limit IS A `Time limit`
      start IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day that is` limit `before` start MEANS
    CONSIDER limit's unit
    WHEN Days   THEN start MINUS limit's `number of units`
    WHEN Months THEN `add months` start (0 MINUS limit's `number of units`)
    WHEN Years  THEN `add years` start (0 MINUS limit's `number of units`)

-- The number to give a regulative rule's WITHIN, which counts days.
GIVEN a IS A DATE
      b IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the number of days from` a `to` b MEANS
    Day b MINUS Day a

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rounding of money. "rounded ... to the next higher multiple of four dollars" and "to the next lower
-- multiple of four dollars" (ss 12, 12.1, 22) leave a number that is already a multiple where it is.

GIVEN x IS A NUMBER
      m IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
x `rounded to the next higher multiple of` m MEANS
    CEILING (x DIVIDED BY m) TIMES m

GIVEN x IS A NUMBER
      m IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
x `rounded to the next lower multiple of` m MEANS
    FLOOR (x DIVIDED BY m) TIMES m

-- Regulations ss 7 and 8(2): where a fraction of a dollar has three or more digits, a third digit of
-- 5 or more raises the second digit by one and a lesser third digit is simply dropped, and the third and
-- later digits are dropped. On an exact number that is rounding half up, to the cent. Regulations
-- ss 8(3) and 9(2) apply the same dropped-digit rule to three and to one decimal place.
GIVEN x IS A NUMBER
      n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
x `rounded half up to the number of decimal places` n MEANS
    FLOOR ((x TIMES EXPONENT 10 n) PLUS 0.5) DIVIDED BY EXPONENT 10 n

GIVEN x IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`rounded to the nearest cent` x MEANS
    x `rounded half up to the number of decimal places` 2
